 why?</em></p>
<hr data-start=3D"217" data-end=3D"220">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"niqskd" data-start=3D"222" data-end=3D"239">=E2=9A=
=A1 Your example</h2>
<ul data-start=3D"240" data-end=3D"314">
<li data-section-id=3D"lu42si" data-start=3D"240" data-end=3D"277">
<p data-start=3D"242" data-end=3D"277"><strong data-start=3D"242" data-end=
=3D"270">Undervoltage protection:</strong> 3.2V</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"arb89h" data-start=3D"278" data-end=3D"314">
<p data-start=3D"280" data-end=3D"314"><strong data-start=3D"280" data-end=
=3D"306">Undervoltage recovery:</strong> 3.36V</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"316" data-end=3D"360">=F0=9F=91=89 This is actually <stron=
g data-start=3D"336" data-end=3D"360">correct and sensible</strong></p>
<p data-start=3D"362" data-end=3D"370">Because:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"371" data-end=3D"497">
<li data-section-id=3D"1ihqews" data-start=3D"371" data-end=3D"420">
<p data-start=3D"373" data-end=3D"420">3.2V =3D cutoff (battery is consider=
ed =E2=80=9Ctoo low=E2=80=9D)</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"fx25mm" data-start=3D"421" data-end=3D"497">
<p data-start=3D"423" data-end=3D"497">3.36V =3D battery has recovered enou=
gh to safely resume charging/discharging</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"499" data-end=3D"502">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"1y56obv" data-start=3D"504" data-end=3D"538">=F0=9F=
=A7=A0 The rule (important concept)</h2>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"k9zoex" data-start=3D"540" data-end=3D"586">For unde=
rvoltage (LOW voltage protection):</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"587" data-end=3D"655">
<li data-section-id=3D"vr78iq" data-start=3D"587" data-end=3D"621">
<p data-start=3D"589" data-end=3D"621">Protection =3D <strong data-start=3D=
"602" data-end=3D"621">LOWER threshold</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"a6lkjc" data-start=3D"622" data-end=3D"655">
<p data-start=3D"624" data-end=3D"655">Recovery =3D <strong data-start=3D"6=
35" data-end=3D"655">HIGHER threshold</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"657" data-end=3D"675">=E2=9C=94 Example pattern:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"676" data-end=3D"751">
<li data-section-id=3D"7avfpv" data-start=3D"676" data-end=3D"717">
<p data-start=3D"678" data-end=3D"717">Cutoff: 3.0=E2=80=933.2V (depends on=
 chemistry)</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1gk6ksx" data-start=3D"718" data-end=3D"751">
<p data-start=3D"720" data-end=3D"751">Recovery: +0.1V to +0.3V higher</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"753" data-end=3D"761">So your:</p>
<blockquote data-start=3D"762" data-end=3D"789">
<p data-start=3D"764" data-end=3D"789">3.2V =E2=86=92 3.36V (+0.16V gap)</p=
>
</blockquote>
<p data-start=3D"791" data-end=3D"834">=E2=9C=94 That is <strong data-start=
=3D"801" data-end=3D"834">normal and healthy hysteresis</strong></p>
<hr data-start=3D"836" data-end=3D"839">
<h3 data-section-id=3D"1s8o83p" data-start=3D"841" data-end=3D"887">For ove=
rvoltage (HIGH voltage protection):</h3>
<p data-start=3D"888" data-end=3D"909">This flips logically:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"911" data-end=3D"979">
<li data-section-id=3D"16xbwxk" data-start=3D"911" data-end=3D"946">
<p data-start=3D"913" data-end=3D"946">Protection =3D <strong data-start=3D=
"926" data-end=3D"946">HIGHER threshold</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1hkq71e" data-start=3D"947" data-end=3D"979">
<p data-start=3D"949" data-end=3D"979">Recovery =3D <strong data-start=3D"9=
60" data-end=3D"979">LOWER threshold</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"981" data-end=3D"989">Example:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"990" data-end=3D"1039">
<li data-section-id=3D"njrhlc" data-start=3D"990" data-end=3D"1021">
<p data-start=3D"992" data-end=3D"1021">Overvoltage protection: 4.10V</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1id6pyw" data-start=3D"1022" data-end=3D"1039">
<p data-start=3D"1024" data-end=3D"1039">Recovery: 4.05V</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1041" data-end=3D"1078">=E2=9C=94 Also correct (same hyste=
resis idea)</p>
<hr data-start=3D"1080" data-end=3D"1083">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"mm4plz" data-start=3D"1085" data-end=3D"1130">=F0=9F=
=94=81 Why recovery is NOT equal to protection</h2>
<p data-start=3D"1131" data-end=3D"1163">Without a gap, the system would:</=
p>
<ul data-start=3D"1164" data-end=3D"1249">
<li data-section-id=3D"1pesge2" data-start=3D"1164" data-end=3D"1174">
<p data-start=3D"1166" data-end=3D"1174">turn off</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1ht8o7l" data-start=3D"1175" data-end=3D"1199">
<p data-start=3D"1177" data-end=3D"1199">instantly turn back on</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"189hxfx" data-start=3D"1200" data-end=3D"1249">
<p data-start=3D"1202" data-end=3D"1249">flicker endlessly (bad for cells +=
 BMS MOSFETs)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1251" data-end=3D"1270">This gap is called:</p>
<blockquote data-start=3D"1271" data-end=3D"1287">
<p data-start=3D"1273" data-end=3D"1287"><strong data-start=3D"1273" data-e=
nd=3D"1287">hysteresis</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start=3D"1289" data-end=3D"1292">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"i7ii30" data-start=3D"1294" data-end=3D"1336">=F0=9F=
=94=8B What matters more than exact numbers</h2>
<p data-start=3D"1338" data-end=3D"1389">For lithium-ion (typical Li-ion / =
LiFePO=E2=82=84 systems):</p>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"6bo55t" data-start=3D"1391" data-end=3D"1435">Underv=
oltage (important safety guidance)</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1436" data-end=3D"1518">
<li data-section-id=3D"1i9fq8v" data-start=3D"1436" data-end=3D"1518">
<p data-start=3D"1438" data-end=3D"1453">Don=E2=80=99t go below:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1456" data-end=3D"1518">
<li data-section-id=3D"fepycr" data-start=3D"1456" data-end=3D"1475">
<p data-start=3D"1458" data-end=3D"1475">Li-ion: ~3.0=E2=80=933.2V</p>
